const CourseLectures=[{"id":"foundations","title":"From a problem to a model","subtitle":"Chapter 1","outcomes":["Translate a decision into variables, an objective and constraints.","Use break-even and airline-pricing models to compare alternatives."],"topics":[{"id":"modeling","title":"The management science approach","body":"Management science uses mathematical models and evidence to support decisions. The course also calls it operations research, quantitative methods and business analytics. Pricing, production, logistics, scheduling and ranking all fit this approach.\nStart by observing a problem. Define the decision, its boundaries and the organization’s objective. Construct a model; solve it; then implement and check the decision in its actual setting. A mathematically optimal answer is useful only if the model represents the real problem.\nA decision variable is something you choose. A parameter is an input you treat as given. The objective measures the result you want to maximize or minimize; constraints define the permitted choices.","sections":[{"title":"The steel-product example","body":"Let x be the number of products. Each sells for $20, costs $5 to produce and uses 4 lb of steel. There are 100 lb available.\n$$\\max Z=(20-5)x=15x,\\qquad 4x\\le100,\\quad x\\ge0.$$\nProfit rises with x, so the resource constraint binds: x = 25 and Z = $375. The slides write the binding constraint as an equality; the availability statement itself is an upper bound. With a positive contribution and no demand restriction, both give the same optimum."}],"sources":["chapter01.pdf:7-12"],"check":{"question":"Which is a decision variable in the steel example?","options":["The 100 lb available","The number of products made","The $20 selling price"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The model chooses production. Steel availability and selling price are parameters."}},{"id":"break-even","title":"Break-even: cost, revenue and profit","body":"Fixed cost does not change with volume over the modeled range. Variable cost is the cost of one more unit. Let F be fixed cost, c the cost per unit, p the selling price and v the number of units sold.\n$$TC=F+cv,\\qquad R=pv,\\qquad Z=(p-c)v-F.$$\nAt break-even, revenue equals total cost and profit is zero. When p > c:\n$$v_{BE}=\\frac{F}{p-c}.$$\nIf p = c and F > 0, no finite sales volume covers fixed cost. If p < c, each additional sale increases the loss. The simple formula also assumes that production can be sold at the stated price.","sections":[{"title":"Western Clothing Company","body":"With F = $10,000, c = $8 and p = $23 per pair, the contribution is $15 per pair. Break-even is 10,000/15 = 666.67 pairs. For whole pairs, at least 667 sales are needed for a nonnegative profit; at 667 pairs the profit is $5.\nThe revenue line starts at zero; the cost line starts at fixed cost. Their crossing divides loss from profit. Higher fixed cost shifts the cost line up; higher unit cost steepens it. A higher price steepens revenue, but may also reduce demand or require better quality and a higher cost."}],"sources":["chapter01.pdf:13-21"],"warning":"Chapter 1, page 15 prints a minus sign in total cost. Fixed and variable costs add: TC = F + cv.","experiment":"break-even"},{"id":"airline-demand","title":"Airline demand and a single fare","body":"The airline has 130 seats per departure and a full economy fare of $60. Its observed (fare, tickets sold) pairs are (40,55), (50,40), (30,102) and (38,60). A fitted curve describes expected demand; it need not pass through every observation.\nThe lecture’s demand model has a kink at price $40, where expected demand is 50. Demand is nonnegative and sales cannot exceed capacity.\n$$q(p)=\\begin{cases}250-5p&0\\le p\\le40\\\\90-p&40\\le p\\le90\\\\0&p\\ge90.\\end{cases}$$\n$$s(p)=\\min\\{130,q(p)\\},\\qquad R(p)=p\\,s(p).$$","sections":[{"title":"Compare the first three strategies","body":"At $60, 30 tickets sell and revenue is $1,800. To fill the plane, solve 250 − 5p = 130, giving p = $24 and revenue $3,120.\nIn the uncapped low-price region, R = 250p − 5p²; its derivative is 250 − 10p. The stationary point is p = $25, with 125 passengers and revenue $3,125. On the higher-price branch, p = $45 gives 45 passengers and $2,025. Checking both branches and their boundaries shows that $25 is the best common fare.\nFilling every seat is therefore not the same objective as maximizing revenue. The small difference here makes that distinction concrete."}],"sources":["chapter01.pdf:22-25","chapter01.pdf:28"],"experiment":"airline"},{"id":"segmentation","title":"Two passenger types and two fares","body":"The lecture separates business travelers who purchase within 14 days of departure from leisure travelers who purchase at least 15 days ahead. The observed business/leisure counts are 52/3 at $40, 40/0 at $50, 65/37 at $30 and 57/8 at $38.\n$$q_1(p_1)=\\max(0,90-p_1),\\qquad q_2(p_2)=\\max(0,160-4p_2).$$\nUnder a single fare, these demands add to the piecewise total-demand curve. Under separate fares, optimize the two revenues subject to the shared 130-seat capacity.","sections":[{"title":"Strategy 4: optimize each segment","body":"Ignoring capacity initially, business revenue is p₁(90 − p₁), maximized at p₁ = 45 and q₁ = 45. Leisure revenue is p₂(160 − 4p₂), maximized at p₂ = 20 and q₂ = 80. Together they use 125 seats, so capacity does not alter this solution.\n$$R=45(45)+20(80)=3625.$$\nThe strategy assumes that the fare restrictions successfully separate the passenger types. If everyone can buy the cheaper fare, this calculation does not describe actual sales."}],"sources":["chapter01.pdf:26-29","chapter01.pdf:33"]},{"id":"app-fare","title":"App specials and a binding seat constraint","body":"Keep a full fare of $60 for 30 business passengers. A separate app fare a is offered to additional price-sensitive business travelers. Under the slide model, app demand is 60 − a; leisure demand at fare b is 160 − 4b.\n$$R=1800+a(60-a)+b(160-4b).$$\n$$30+(60-a)+(160-4b)\\le130\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a+4b\\ge120.$$\nThe derivation assumes that the 30 full-fare passengers keep paying $60; it does not model them switching to the app.","sections":[{"title":"Strategy 5: hold the leisure fare at $20","body":"There are 80 leisure passengers and 30 full-fare passengers, leaving 20 seats for the app. Capacity requires a ≥ 40. Since app revenue decreases above its unconstrained optimum a = 30, choose a = 40. Revenue is 1,800 + 40×20 + 20×80 = $4,200.\nWithout the app, the $60/$20 pair sells 110 tickets and earns $3,400. This is the intermediate example on page 30."},{"title":"Strategy 6: optimize both discounted fares","body":"The unconstrained optima a = 30 and b = 20 would require 140 seats. Capacity therefore binds. Substitute a = 120 − 4b into revenue:\n$$R(b)=-5400+880b-20b^2.$$\nIts maximum is b = 22, so a = 32. The passenger counts are 30 full-fare, 28 app and 72 leisure passengers: exactly 130. Revenue is 1,800 + 896 + 1,584 = $4,280."}],"sources":["chapter01.pdf:30-33"]},{"id":"decision-support","title":"Methods and decision support systems","body":"Linear programming uses a clear objective, resource restrictions and known parameters. Probabilistic methods explicitly represent uncertainty. Network methods express relationships as nodes and connections and may be deterministic or probabilistic. Forecasting, inventory models, simulation and multicriteria methods address other structures.\nApplications in the slides include scheduling, production planning, inventory, pricing, product assortment, capital budgeting, delivery routes and satellite scheduling. The INFORMS journals on applied analytics and data science provide examples of implementation.\nA decision support system combines a user interface, databases and management-science models. The decision maker asks “what if?”, compares alternatives and examines sensitivity. ERP systems illustrate how these tools connect across organizational functions and internet-based operations.","sections":[],"sources":["chapter01.pdf:34-44"]},{"id":"gantt","title":"Case study: making scheduling results usable","body":"The Mitsubishi production-scheduling example illustrates that a solution also needs a usable interface. The old tool needed about two hours to draw 100,000 schedule items, around 13 items per second. Users also needed to move and resize bars, change colors and hatching, show labels and tooltips, change the time scale, open nested rows and resize columns.\nThe targets were 20,000 items within 10–20 seconds and 100,000 within 0.1–0.2 hour. Instead of preparing the entire chart before displaying anything, compute and draw the requested portion immediately.\nA block is a group of schedule data; the view is the visible region; an extended view caches surrounding content. When the view changes, refresh the screen and cache as needed. Modifications update the local scheduling cache and then the database. The interface, drawing/data-management layer and cache separate presentation from server storage.","sections":[{"title":"Reported evaluation","body":"The prototype used VB.NET with a SQL Server database. Five tests were run in each size group. Group A contained about 2,000 rows and 20,000 items; its mean was 17.6 seconds with less than 100 MB peak memory. Group B contained about 10,000 rows and 100,000 items; its mean was 131 seconds with less than 200 MB.\nThe larger test achieved approximately 763 items per second, roughly 60 times the old throughput. These are historical results reported in the slides, not current hardware benchmarks. The modeling lesson is to design implementation around the user’s actual query, rather than processing everything eagerly."}],"sources":["chapter01.pdf:45-56"]}]},{"id":"linear-programming","title":"Formulate and solve a linear program","subtitle":"Chapter 2 · Geometry","outcomes":["Build resource, requirement and mixture constraints.","Read a feasible region and distinguish optimal, infeasible and unbounded models."],"topics":[{"id":"lp-components","title":"The four assumptions of linear programming","body":"An LP consists of decision variables, a linear objective, linear constraints and variable bounds. The coefficients and right-hand-side values are parameters. To apply it, identify the decision, formulate the model, solve it and implement the result.\nProportionality means a coefficient stays constant as the activity level changes. Additivity means contributions combine by addition, without interaction terms. Divisibility allows fractional variable values. Certainty treats the model’s inputs as known.\n$$\\max\\; c^Tx\\quad\\text{subject to }Ax\\le b,\\;x\\ge0.$$\nThis compact form is an example, not a restriction: an LP can minimize, include equalities or lower-bound requirements, and have other variable bounds. Products of decision variables, squared variables and variable denominators are not linear.\nFor every variable, state its unit and time period. Every term in one constraint must have the same unit. If actual choices must be whole numbers, an integer model may be required; rounding an LP solution can break feasibility.","sections":[],"sources":["chapter02 - part 2.pdf:1-5","chapter02 - part 2.pdf:11"],"check":{"question":"Which expression is linear in x and y?","options":["3x + 2y","xy + 2","x² + y"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Coefficients may be constants, but products and powers of decision variables are not linear."}},{"id":"pottery","title":"Beaver Creek: bowls and mugs","body":"Let x₁ be bowls per day and x₂ mugs per day. Each bowl earns $40, uses one labor hour and 4 lb of clay. Each mug earns $50, uses two hours and 3 lb of clay. There are 40 labor hours and 120 lb of clay available per day.\n$$\\max Z=40x_1+50x_2$$\n$$x_1+2x_2\\le40,\\qquad4x_1+3x_2\\le120,\\qquad x_1,x_2\\ge0.$$\nAt (5,10), labor use is 25 and clay use is 50: 4×5 + 3×10 = 50 lb. Both limits are satisfied and profit is $700. The slides print 70 for that clay sum; the correct total is 50.\nAt (10,20), labor use is 50, exceeding 40, so the plan is infeasible even though its nominal profit is $1,400. Feasibility requires every constraint, including bounds, to hold.","sections":[{"title":"Slack and binding constraints","body":"For an upper-bound resource, slack equals availability minus usage. At (5,10), labor slack is 15 and clay slack is 70. A constraint binds when slack is zero. A feasible plan need not be optimal, and a high objective value does not rescue an infeasible plan."}],"sources":["chapter02 - part 2.pdf:6-8","chapter02 - part 2.pdf:12-14"],"warning":"Several slides repeat 4x₂ + 3x₂ ≤ 120. The product data and graphs establish the intended clay constraint as 4x₁ + 3x₂ ≤ 120."},{"id":"graphical","title":"Graphical solution and corner points","body":"With two variables, draw each boundary line, choose its permitted half-plane and intersect all half-planes with the variable bounds. For a “≤” resource constraint, the origin is often a convenient test point. For a “≥” requirement, the permitted side may point away from the origin.\nIn the pottery model the feasible vertices are (0,0), (0,20), (24,8) and (30,0). Evaluate the objective at every vertex: $0, $1,000, $1,360 and $1,200. The best point is 24 bowls and 8 mugs.","sections":[{"title":"Solve the two binding equations","body":"$$x_1+2x_2=40,\\qquad4x_1+3x_2=120.$$\nMultiply the first equation by four and subtract the second: 5x₂ = 40, hence x₂ = 8 and x₁ = 24. Both resources are exhausted.\nA line of constant profit has slope −c₁/c₂. Move it parallel in the direction of increasing profit until it last touches the feasible set. A finite optimum in this nonnegative, pointed feasible region occurs at a vertex; an entire edge can also be optimal when the objective is parallel to it."}],"sources":["chapter02 - part 2.pdf:14"],"experiment":"pottery","check":{"question":"At the standard pottery optimum, how much labor remains?","options":["0 hours","8 hours","16 hours"],"answer":0,"explanation":"24 + 2×8 = 40, so the labor constraint binds."}},{"id":"fertilizer","title":"Fertilizer: a minimization model","body":"Let x₁ be bags of Super-gro and x₂ bags of Crop-quick. Their costs are $6 and $3. Nitrogen per bag is 2 and 4 lb; phosphate per bag is 4 and 3 lb. The field needs at least 16 lb nitrogen and 24 lb phosphate.\n$$\\min Z=6x_1+3x_2$$\n$$2x_1+4x_2\\ge16,\\qquad4x_1+3x_2\\ge24,\\qquad x_1,x_2\\ge0.$$\nThe feasible region extends upward and rightward. Its lower boundary has vertices (0,8), (4.8,1.6) and (8,0). The costs are $24, $33.60 and $48. Buy 8 bags of Crop-quick and none of Super-gro.\nAt this solution, nitrogen supplied is 32 lb, so surplus above its minimum is 16 lb; phosphate is exactly 24 lb. An unbounded feasible region can still have a finite optimal value.","sections":[],"sources":["chapter02 - part 2.pdf:9-10","chapter02 - part 2.pdf:15"]},{"id":"irregular","title":"Multiple optima, infeasibility and unboundedness","body":"Change pottery profits to 70 for bowls and 20 for mugs. The vertex profits are 0, 400, 1,840 and 2,100; (30,0) becomes optimal. Changing the objective changes which feasible point is best, without changing feasibility.\nFor profits 40 and 30, the objective is ten times the clay expression. Every feasible point on the clay boundary between (24,8) and (30,0) earns $1,200. These are alternate optima, including all convex combinations of the endpoints.","sections":[{"title":"An infeasible model","body":"$$4x_1+2x_2\\le8,\\qquad x_1\\ge4,\\qquad x_2\\ge6.$$\nThe lower bounds force the left side to be at least 28, contradicting the upper bound 8. No choice satisfies every restriction; there is no feasible solution to optimize."},{"title":"An unbounded objective","body":"$$\\max(4x_1+2x_2),\\qquad x_1\\ge4,\\quad0\\le x_2\\le2.$$\nIncreasing x₁ indefinitely stays feasible and increases profit without limit. This is an unbounded maximization problem. It often signals an omitted resource restriction, but whether one is missing must be decided from the original problem."}],"sources":["chapter02 - part 2.pdf:16-20"]},{"id":"hot-dogs","title":"Mixture constraints: chicken and beef","body":"A 1,000 lb batch contains chicken at $3/lb and beef at $5/lb. It needs at least 500 lb chicken, at least 200 lb beef, and a chicken-to-beef ratio of at least two to one.\nLet c and b denote pounds of the two ingredients.\n$$\\min Z=3c+5b,\\quad c+b=1000,\\quad c\\ge500,\\quad b\\ge200,\\quad c\\ge2b.$$\nUse c = 1,000 − b. The constraints imply 200 ≤ b ≤ 1,000/3, and cost is 3,000 + 2b. Choose the smallest feasible beef amount: b = 200 and c = 800. The cost is $3,400.\nThe feasible set is a line segment because total batch weight is an equality. Write the ratio as c ≥ 2b; this keeps the constraint linear and also handles a zero denominator cleanly.","sections":[],"sources":["chapter02 - part 2.pdf:21-23"]},{"id":"furniture","title":"Furniture production and a second graph","body":"Let x₁ be doors and x₂ windows. Workshop 1 allows four door-hours, workshop 2 allows 12 hours with two per window, and workshop 3 allows 18 hours with three per door and two per window. Profit is $300 per door and $500 per window.\n$$\\max Z=300x_1+500x_2,\\quad x_1\\le4,\\quad2x_2\\le12,\\quad3x_1+2x_2\\le18,\\quad x\\ge0.$$\nThe vertices are (0,0), (4,0), (4,3), (2,6), (0,6). Profits are 0, 1,200, 2,700, 3,600 and 3,000. The optimal mix is two doors and six windows, using all hours in workshops 2 and 3, and leaving two hours in workshop 1.","sections":[{"title":"Final graphical exercise","body":"$$\\max Z=4x_1+5x_2,\\quad x_1+2x_2\\le10,\\quad6x_1+6x_2\\le36,\\quad x_1\\le4,\\quad x\\ge0.$$\nDivide the second constraint by six. The vertices are (0,0), (4,0), (4,2), (2,4), (0,5), with objective values 0, 16, 26, 28 and 25. The optimum is (2,4), Z = 28. The x₁ upper bound has slack 2."}],"sources":["chapter02-part 1.pdf:24-27","chapter02 - part 2.pdf:24","chapter02 - part 2.pdf:41-42"]}]},{"id":"sensitivity","title":"What changes the best decision?","subtitle":"Chapter 2 · Sensitivity","outcomes":["Find objective-coefficient ranges and local shadow prices.","Distinguish changes in prices, resources and technology."],"topics":[{"id":"objective-range","title":"Changing objective coefficients","body":"Objective sensitivity asks how much a coefficient can change while the current optimal point remains optimal. For the pottery intersection (24,8), the objective slope must lie between the slopes of its two adjacent edges, −1/2 and −4/3.\n$$\\frac12\\le\\frac{c_1}{c_2}\\le\\frac43\\quad(c_2>0).$$\nHold c₂ = 50: 25 ≤ c₁ ≤ 200/3 ≈ 66.67. Hold c₁ = 40: 30 ≤ c₂ ≤ 80. At either endpoint the current point is still optimal, but an adjacent edge also becomes optimal. These are one-at-a-time ranges; they cannot be applied independently to simultaneous coefficient changes.","sections":[{"title":"An algebraic derivation","body":"The value of one labor hour is yL and one pound of clay is yC. With both pottery products produced, their profits equal the imputed resource costs:\n$$y_L+4y_C=c_1,\\quad2y_L+3y_C=c_2.$$\n$$y_L=\\frac{4c_2-3c_1}{5},\\qquad y_C=\\frac{2c_1-c_2}{5}.$$\nRequiring both values to be nonnegative gives the same objective-coefficient range. At the original profits, yL = 16 and yC = 6."}],"sources":["chapter02 - part 2.pdf:25-27","chapter02 - part 2.pdf:30"],"experiment":"profit"},{"id":"fertilizer-range","title":"Cost sensitivity and reduced cost","body":"At the fertilizer optimum (0,8), the adjacent edge toward (4.8,1.6) has slope −4/3. The point remains optimal when c₂ ≥ 0 and c₁ ≥ (4/3)c₂. Holding c₂ = 3 gives c₁ ≥ 4. Holding c₁ = 6 gives 0 ≤ c₂ ≤ 4.5.\nAt c₁ = 4 or c₂ = 4.5, a whole boundary edge has the same cost. At c₂ = 0 and c₁ positive, any feasible point with x₁ = 0 has zero cost. If c₂ becomes negative, buying arbitrarily many Crop-quick bags makes this unbounded minimization model decrease without limit.\nReduced cost measures the objective penalty of bringing a currently zero variable into the solution, under the current optimal basis. For Super-gro, one bag costs $6 but replaces $4 of Crop-quick when phosphate remains binding. Its reduced cost is 2, and its cost must fall by $2 before it can enter without worsening the objective.","sections":[],"sources":["chapter02 - part 2.pdf:28-29","chapter02 - part 2.pdf:31","chapter03-2.pdf:12"]},{"id":"labor-shadow","title":"Labor availability and shadow price","body":"An RHS sensitivity range preserves the optimal basis, not the numerical values of the decision variables. With pottery clay fixed at 120 lb and labor availability L, solve the same two binding constraints:\n$$x_1=48-0.6L,\\qquad x_2=0.8L-24.$$\nBoth variables remain nonnegative for 30 ≤ L ≤ 80. Within this range:\n$$Z^*(L)=720+16L.$$\nThe shadow price is $16 per additional labor hour. From 40 to 60 hours, profit rises by 20×16 = $320 to $1,680, with (12,24). At 30 hours, profit is $1,200 with (30,0). This marginal value is local; it is not a purchase recommendation without considering the cost of acquiring more labor.","sections":[{"title":"The full value function","body":"$$Z^*(L)=\\begin{cases}40L&0\\le L\\le30\\\\720+16L&30\\le L\\le80\\\\2000&L\\ge80.\\end{cases}$$\nFor scarce labor, make only bowls because they earn more per labor hour. Above 80 hours, clay limits production to 40 mugs and extra labor has no value. The successive marginal values are 40, 16 and 0; at the breakpoints the left and right derivatives differ."}],"sources":["chapter02 - part 2.pdf:32-34","chapter02 - part 2.pdf:37"],"experiment":"labor","check":{"question":"Does the current mix (24,8) stay numerically unchanged when labor increases to 41 hours?","options":["Yes","No: the basis stays, but quantities change"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The new quantities are 23.4 bowls and 8.8 mugs. Profit rises by $16."}},{"id":"clay-shadow","title":"Clay availability and the value of a resource","body":"Keep labor at 40 hours and let clay availability be C. At the intersection:\n$$x_1=\\frac{2C-120}{5},\\qquad x_2=\\frac{160-C}{5}.$$\nThe basis remains feasible for 60 ≤ C ≤ 160, and optimal profit is 640 + 6C. Thus the shadow price at C = 120 is $6 per pound. Reducing clay to 80 lowers profit by $240 to $1,120; the new mix is (8,16).","sections":[{"title":"Beyond the local range","body":"$$Z^*(C)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{50}{3}C&0\\le C\\le60\\\\640+6C&60\\le C\\le160\\\\1600&C\\ge160.\\end{cases}$$\nWith little clay, mugs earn more per pound of clay. With abundant clay, labor limits production to 40 bowls. The successive marginal values are 50/3, 6 and 0 dollars per pound."}],"sources":["chapter02 - part 2.pdf:35","chapter02 - part 2.pdf:37"]},{"id":"nutrient-shadow","title":"Requirement sensitivity in a minimum-cost model","body":"A higher nutrient minimum can raise the least achievable cost. At the current fertilizer solution, nitrogen is already oversupplied; its shadow price is zero. Phosphate binds and its shadow price is $1 per additional pound.\nCrop-quick is cheaper per pound of both nutrients. For any nonnegative nitrogen requirement N and phosphate requirement P, the optimal solution uses only Crop-quick:\n$$x_1^*=0,\\quad x_2^*=\\max\\left(\\frac N4,\\frac P3\\right),\\quad Z^*=\\max\\left(\\frac{3N}{4},P\\right).$$\nHold P = 24. Cost stays 24 until N reaches 32, then rises at $0.75 per pound of required nitrogen. Hold N = 16. Cost stays 12 until P reaches 12, then rises at $1 per pound of required phosphate. At N = 16, P = 24, nitrogen can increase by 16 before its shadow price changes; phosphate can decrease by 12 before its current shadow price changes.\nFor nonnegative requirements, the current nitrogen range is 0–32 and the phosphate range is 12 upward. A solver can report an effectively infinite allowable decrease for a redundant nitrogen lower bound; negative nutrient requirements have no physical meaning here.","sections":[],"sources":["chapter02 - part 2.pdf:36","chapter02 - part 2.pdf:38","chapter03-2.pdf:12"]},{"id":"new-product","title":"A new product and a second factory","body":"A cup would earn $30, using 1.2 labor hours and 2 lb of clay. At the current shadow prices its resources are worth 1.2×16 + 2×6 = $31.20. Its net contribution relative to these scarce resources is −$1.20, so adding it cannot improve the original optimum.\n$$\\max40x_1+50x_2+30x_3$$\n$$x_1+2x_2+1.2x_3\\le40,\\qquad4x_1+3x_2+2x_3\\le120,\\quad x\\ge0.$$\nAt profit 31.20, producing some cups can become an alternate optimum; above that threshold re-solve the model. The shadow-price test is based on the current model and its resource tradeoffs.","sections":[{"title":"Willow Valley comparison","body":"The second factory’s displayed model uses profits 48 and 60, labor coefficients 1 and 3, and clay coefficients 4 and 3. With labor limit H and clay 120, the intersection is y₁ = (120 − H)/3 and y₂ = (4H − 120)/9, valid for 30 ≤ H ≤ 120.\n$$Z_W^*(H)=1120+\\frac{32}{3}H.$$\nIn this region Willow’s labor shadow price is 32/3 and its clay shadow price is 28/3. Compare marginal resource values, not just unit profits. If H = 40, the intersection is (80/3,40/9), with profit 4,640/3. If H = 65, it is (55/3,140/9), with profit 5,440/3."}],"sources":["chapter02 - part 2.pdf:39-40"],"warning":"The labor RHS in the second-factory image on page 39 is overwritten in red and is not unambiguous. The derivation uses H and shows both 40 and 65 rather than choosing an undocumented value."},{"id":"technology","title":"Furniture sensitivity and changing technology","body":"For the furniture solution (2,6), hold the window profit at 500. Door profit can range from 0 to 750 while that point remains optimal. At zero, producing fewer doors is equally good; at 750, the workshop-3 boundary edge becomes equally profitable.\nLet workshop 3 have H hours. Between 12 and 24 hours, windows remain 6 and doors are (H − 12)/3. The profit is 1,800 + 100H, so workshop 3 has a shadow price of $100 per hour. Its original 18 hours can decrease by 6 or increase by 6 within this basis.\nFor H below 12, only windows are made and the marginal value is $250 per hour; above 24, workshops 1 and 2 cap output at (4,6), so extra workshop-3 time has zero value.","sections":[{"title":"Changing a coefficient inside a constraint","body":"If pottery labor per bowl changes from 1 to α, the labor boundary is αx₁ + 2x₂ ≤ 40. This rotates the line rather than shifting it in parallel. Objective and RHS sensitivity ranges do not answer this change directly.\nThe slides illustrate α ≈ 1.33 and label the bowl-only point (30,0). At α = 4/3 exactly, the solution is (30,0), Z = 1,200. If 1.33 is interpreted literally, the intersection is approximately (29.9252,0.0998), with Z ≈ 1,201.995. Preserve the distinction between a rounded coefficient and its exact value."}],"sources":["chapter02 - part 2.pdf:43-46"]}]},{"id":"computer-solution","title":"From a model to a solver","subtitle":"Chapter 3","outcomes":["Build a spreadsheet model and read its sensitivity report.","Write an LP file and interpret a solver result critically."],"topics":[{"id":"spreadsheet","title":"Build the pottery model in Excel","body":"Computer solvers automate procedures such as the simplex method. The slides introduce Excel Solver and Cardinal Optimizer (COPT), alongside examples of other optimization packages. Software solves the formulation supplied to it; it cannot check whether the coefficients express the intended business problem.\nSeparate inputs, decision cells, objective and constraint calculations. In the slide spreadsheet, C2:D2 contains profits 40 and 50; C3:D3 labor coefficients 1 and 2; C4:D4 clay coefficients 4 and 3. C6 contains 40 and C7 contains 120. C11:D11 holds the changing production quantities.\nSet C15 to =SUMPRODUCT(C11:D11,C2:D2), C19 to =SUMPRODUCT(C3:D3,C11:D11), and C20 to =SUMPRODUCT(C4:D4,C11:D11). E19 links to C6 and E20 to C7.\nIn the demonstrated Solver setup, maximize C15 by changing C11:D11, subject to C19:C20 ≤ E19:E20. Apply nonnegative bounds and use the Simplex LP method. Solve, then independently substitute the returned values into every original constraint.\nThe expected result is (24,8), profit 1,360, labor usage 40 and clay usage 120. The Answer Report marks both resource constraints binding with zero slack. The original values shown in a report are the initial guesses, not a second optimum.","sections":[],"sources":["chapter03-2.pdf:1-7"]},{"id":"reports","title":"Read sensitivity reports without overextending them","body":"An objective-coefficient range is current coefficient minus allowable decrease through current coefficient plus allowable increase. For pottery bowls, 40 − 15 = 25 and 40 + 26.6667 = 66.6667. For mugs, 50 − 20 = 30 and 50 + 30 = 80. Both produced variables have reduced cost zero.\nThe labor report gives RHS 40, shadow price 16, allowable decrease 10 and increase 40: range 30–80. Clay gives RHS 120, shadow price 6, decrease 60 and increase 40: range 60–160. Within a range, the optimal quantities can change even though the basis is retained.\nFor fertilizer, the changing cells are C9:C10. Minimize C13 = SUMPRODUCT(C2:C3,C9:C10). The nutrient left sides are SUMPRODUCT(C9:C10,D2:D3) and SUMPRODUCT(C9:C10,E2:E3), each constrained above its requirement. The answer is (0,8), total cost 24.\nThe fertilizer report gives Super-gro reduced cost 2, cost coefficient 6 and allowable decrease 2; Crop-quick coefficient 3, increase 1.5 and decrease 3. Nitrogen’s shadow price is 0 with surplus 16; phosphate’s is 1. Large entries such as 1E+30 denote an effectively unbounded allowable change in the software’s reporting convention.\nOne-at-a-time sensitivity reports are local and conditional. Integer restrictions, changed coefficients in the constraint matrix or simultaneous data changes can require solving again.","sections":[],"sources":["chapter03-2.pdf:8-12"]},{"id":"aircraft","title":"Aircraft parts: verify a numerical solution","body":"Let x₁ and x₂ be production quantities of the two aircraft parts. The three stages are stamping, drilling and finishing.\n$$\\max Z=650x_1+910x_2$$\n$$4x_1+7.5x_2\\le105,\\quad6.2x_1+4.9x_2\\le90,\\quad9.1x_1+4.1x_2\\le110,\\quad x\\ge0.$$\nSolving the first two boundaries gives x₁ ≈ 5.96654 and x₂ ≈ 10.81784. Profit is approximately $13,722.49. Finishing usage is about 98.64870 hours, leaving about 11.35130 hours. Thus stamping and drilling bind while finishing does not.\nThese are continuous LP quantities. If the decision requires whole aircraft parts, impose integrality and solve the resulting integer model; independently rounding the displayed solution is not justified by this lecture’s LP calculation.","sections":[],"sources":["chapter03-2.pdf:13"]},{"id":"copt","title":"LP files and the COPT command-line workflow","body":"The lecture demonstrates COPT 7.2, including programming interfaces, a command-line optimizer and modeling-tool connectors. It also shows dated solver benchmarks: rankings depend on the instance set, hardware, version and benchmark definition, so they are context rather than a universal ordering.\nA text LP file separates objective sense, objective, named constraints and bounds. The corrected pottery model is included below as selectable text. LP and MPS are alternative model-exchange formats; writing an MPS file does not solve the model.\nIn the illustrated interactive shell: read example1.lp loads the model; opt solves it; writesol example1 saves the solution; writemps example1.mps exports MPS; writelp example1 exports LP. The slides also show set timelimit 10 and writeparam example1 for a 10-second limit and parameter export. Command behavior is described for the lecture’s version.\nOther commands displayed in the course are display/get for parameters and attributes, optlp for an LP solve, iis for an irreducible infeasible subsystem, feasrelax for feasibility relaxation, tune for parameter tuning and loadtuneparam for a tuning result. Use the installed version’s manual for its exact syntax and available options.\nAn IIS helps locate conflicting constraints; a relaxed solution changes the original problem. Neither is permission to discard a genuine business requirement. Always inspect solution status before using the objective and variable values.","sections":[],"sources":["chapter03-2.pdf:14-22"],"warning":"The COPT screenshot actually encodes 4x₂ + 3x₂ ≤ 120 and returns x₁ = 40, Z = 1,600. That solves the mistyped model, not the intended pottery model. The corrected file below returns (24,8), Z = 1,360."}]},{"id":"modeling-examples","title":"A toolkit of modeling patterns","subtitle":"Chapter 4","outcomes":["Formulate product mix, diet, allocation, transport and blending models.","Track inventory, investment timing and relative efficiency."],"topics":[{"id":"shirts","title":"Product mix: printed shirts","body":"Quick-Screen chooses dozens of front-only sweatshirts x₁, front-and-back sweatshirts x₂, front-only T-shirts x₃ and front-and-back T-shirts x₄. A sweatshirt box occupies three standard box spaces; a T-shirt box occupies one. The decision unit is a dozen, not an individual shirt.\n$$\\max Z=90x_1+125x_2+45x_3+65x_4.$$\n$$0.10x_1+0.25x_2+0.08x_3+0.21x_4\\le72$$\n$$3x_1+3x_2+x_3+x_4\\le1200$$\n$$36x_1+48x_2+25x_3+35x_4\\le25000$$\n$$x_1+x_2\\le500,\\qquad x_3+x_4\\le500,\\qquad x_i\\ge0.$$\nThe constraints represent processing hours, one truck’s capacity, production budget, sweatshirt stock and T-shirt stock. Profit and cost are separate coefficients: maximize the stated profit, while production cost consumes budget. Do not subtract the cost a second time from an already net profit.","sections":[],"sources":["chapter04-2.pdf:3-5"]},{"id":"diet","title":"Diet: lower and upper nutrient limits","body":"Let xᵢ be servings of food i in the table, using its stated unit (cup, egg, slice or orange). Fractional servings are permitted in this LP. Minimize total cost subject to minimum calories, iron, calcium, protein and fiber, and maximum fat and cholesterol.\n$$\\min\\sum_{i=1}^{10}c_ix_i,\\qquad x_i\\ge0.$$\nUsing the table’s nutrient coefficients, require calories ≥ 420, iron ≥ 5 mg, calcium ≥ 400 mg, protein ≥ 20 g, fiber ≥ 12 g, fat ≤ 20 g and cholesterol ≤ 30 mg.\nFor example, the calorie constraint starts 90x₁ + 110x₂ + 100x₃ + 90x₄ + 75x₅ and continues with the remaining five table entries. The cholesterol constraint is 270x₅ + 8x₆ + 12x₈ ≤ 30; zero coefficients can be omitted. The objective uses the last column, starting 0.18x₁ + 0.22x₂ + 0.10x₃.\nEach nutrient gets its own row and direction. A low-cost food can be unattractive because it consumes a limited upper-bound nutrient such as fat or cholesterol.","sections":[],"sources":["chapter04-2.pdf:6-7"]},{"id":"portfolio","title":"Investment allocation and ratio restrictions","body":"This is the lecture’s mathematical allocation example. Let x₁, x₂, x₃ and x₄ be dollars in municipal bonds, certificates of deposit, treasury bills and the growth stock fund. Annual returns are 8.5%, 5%, 6.5% and 13%, with total capital $70,000.\n$$\\max Z=0.085x_1+0.05x_2+0.065x_3+0.13x_4.$$\n$$x_1\\le14000,\\qquad x_2\\le x_1+x_3+x_4$$\n$$x_2+x_3\\ge21000,\\qquad x_2+x_3\\ge1.2(x_1+x_4)$$\n$$x_1+x_2+x_3+x_4=70000,\\qquad x_i\\ge0.$$\nThe first restriction caps municipal bonds at 20%. The second limits CDs to the other investments combined. The third puts at least 30% in CDs and treasury bills. The fourth is a ratio restriction comparing the two groups, not “20% of total capital.”\nThe objective is return earned, not final wealth; adding the fixed initial capital would not change the maximizing allocation. This classroom model treats returns as known parameters.","sections":[],"sources":["chapter04-2.pdf:8-9"]},{"id":"advertising","title":"Marketing: maximize exposure within a budget","body":"Let x₁, x₂ and x₃ count television commercials, radio commercials and newspaper ads. Exposures are 20,000, 12,000 and 9,000 people per ad; costs are $15,000, $6,000 and $4,000. Budget is $100,000 and production capacity is 15 ads in total.\n$$\\max Z=20000x_1+12000x_2+9000x_3$$\n$$15000x_1+6000x_2+4000x_3\\le100000$$\n$$x_1\\le4,\\quad x_2\\le10,\\quad x_3\\le7,\\quad x_1+x_2+x_3\\le15,\\quad x_i\\ge0.$$\nThe formulation in the slides is continuous. If ads must be purchased whole, add integer restrictions. The exposure objective is additive as assumed in the data; it does not account for repeated viewers across advertisements.","sections":[],"sources":["chapter04-2.pdf:10-12"]},{"id":"transport","title":"Transportation: supply, demand and unused capacity","body":"Let xᵢⱼ be televisions shipped from warehouse i to store j. Warehouse supplies are 300 in Cincinnati, 200 in Atlanta and 200 in Pittsburgh. Demands are 150 in New York, 250 in Dallas and 200 in Detroit.\n$$\\min\\sum_i\\sum_jc_{ij}x_{ij}.$$\n$$\\sum_jx_{1j}\\le300,\\quad\\sum_jx_{2j}\\le200,\\quad\\sum_jx_{3j}\\le200.$$\n$$\\sum_ix_{iA}=150,\\quad\\sum_ix_{iB}=250,\\quad\\sum_ix_{iC}=200,\\quad x_{ij}\\ge0.$$\nSupply totals 700 and demand totals 600, so 100 units remain at warehouses. If all warehouse rows were forced to equality without an extra destination, the model would be inconsistent. An equivalent balanced formulation adds a dummy destination with demand 100 and zero shipping cost when unused stock has no modeled penalty.\nThe objective coefficients are lane-specific: choosing the cheapest warehouse separately for every store can violate warehouse capacities. The model coordinates those shared limits.","sections":[],"sources":["chapter04-2.pdf:13-15"]},{"id":"blending","title":"Blending: percentages become linear constraints","body":"Let xᵢⱼ be barrels of component i used in grade j, with grades S (super), P (premium) and E (extra). Define qⱼ = x₁ⱼ + x₂ⱼ + x₃ⱼ. Component supplies are 4,500, 2,700 and 3,500 barrels/day; costs are $12, $10 and $14 per barrel. Selling prices are 23, 20 and 18 dollars for S, P and E.\n$$\\max Z=23q_S+20q_P+18q_E-\\sum_j(12x_{1j}+10x_{2j}+14x_{3j}).$$\n$$\\sum_jx_{1j}\\le4500,\\quad\\sum_jx_{2j}\\le2700,\\quad\\sum_jx_{3j}\\le3500.$$\n$$q_S,q_P,q_E\\ge3000,\\qquad x_{ij}\\ge0.$$\nSuper needs x₁S ≥ 0.50qS and x₂S ≤ 0.30qS. Premium needs x₁P ≥ 0.40qP and x₃P ≤ 0.25qP. Extra needs x₁E ≥ 0.60qE and x₂E ≥ 0.10qE.\nAll these restrictions are linear because q is a sum of variables multiplied only by constant percentages. A percentage refers to its own grade’s total, not total company production.\nAt the minimum 3,000 barrels of each grade, component 1 needs at least 1,500 + 1,200 + 1,800 = 4,500 barrels: its entire supply. Consequently each grade must stay at 3,000 and its component-1 minimum must bind.","sections":[{"title":"A feasible optimal blend","body":"Use S = (1,500,900,600), P = (1,200,1,500,300), E = (1,800,300,900), in component order (1,2,3). Totals are 4,500, 2,700 and 1,800. Every grade specification holds. Revenue is 183,000 and ingredient cost is 106,200, leaving profit 76,800.\nBecause component 1 fixes grade totals and component 2 is cheaper than component 3, using all 2,700 available barrels of component 2 minimizes cost. Other allocations with the same totals and satisfied grade restrictions can have the same optimum."}],"sources":["chapter04-2.pdf:16-18"]},{"id":"production","title":"Multiperiod production and inventory","body":"PM Computers can make 160 computers/week in regular time and 50 more with overtime. Unit assembly costs are $190 and $260; carrying a computer to the next week costs $10. Orders over six weeks are 105, 170, 230, 180, 150 and 250.\nLet rₜ, oₜ and Iₜ be regular production, overtime production and ending inventory in week t. With no starting inventory and no backlogs:\n$$I_{t-1}+r_t+o_t=d_t+I_t,\\qquad I_0=0.$$\n$$0\\le r_t\\le160,\\quad0\\le o_t\\le50,\\quad I_t\\ge0.$$\n$$\\min\\;190\\sum_{t=1}^6r_t+260\\sum_{t=1}^6o_t+10\\sum_{t=1}^5 I_t.$$\nWe use zero terminal inventory I₆ = 0 and charge holding for inventory carried between weeks. These boundary assumptions make the formulation explicit; the source gives costs and demands but leaves the model page blank.\nWeek 3 demand exceeds that week’s maximum production by 20; week 6 exceeds it by 40. Earlier inventory is essential. Looking only at total six-week capacity would miss these timing constraints.","sections":[{"title":"A feasible least-cost schedule","body":"Regular production is 160 every week. Use overtime of 0, 0, 25, 20, 30 and 50. Ending inventories are 55, 45, 0, 0, 40 and 0. Check each balance, for example week 3: 45 + 160 + 25 = 230.\nRegular production costs 182,400; overtime costs 32,500; holding costs 1,400. Total cost is $216,300. Regular production is cheaper than overtime even with up to five weeks of holding, so all regular capacity is used. The required 125 overtime units are placed as late as the balance and capacity constraints allow."}],"sources":["chapter04-2.pdf:19-21"]},{"id":"pet-food","title":"Pet food: keep weight and cans consistent","body":"There are 600 lb horse meat, 800 lb fish and 1,000 lb cereal per week, plus 2,250 cans of 16 oz each. A can weighs one pound. Meow Chow must be at least half fish; Bow Chow must be at least half horse meat. Profits are $0.80 and $0.96 per can.\nLet hM, fM, cM be pounds of the three ingredients in Meow Chow, and hB, fB, cB the amounts in Bow Chow. Define M = hM + fM + cM and B = hB + fB + cB. Because each can is one pound, M and B also count cans.\n$$\\max Z=0.80M+0.96B$$\n$$h_M+h_B\\le600,\\quad f_M+f_B\\le800,\\quad c_M+c_B\\le1000$$\n$$M+B\\le2250,\\quad f_M\\ge0.5M,\\quad h_B\\ge0.5B,\\quad h,f,c\\ge0.$$\nHorse meat limits B to 1,200. Since Bow Chow has higher profit and both products have positive profit, a best plan uses 1,200 Bow Chow and 1,050 Meow Chow, earning $1,992. One feasible recipe uses 600 horse + 600 cereal for Bow Chow and 650 fish + 400 cereal for Meow Chow. All 2,250 cans are used; 150 lb fish remain.","sections":[],"sources":["chapter04-2.pdf:22-24"]},{"id":"cash-flow","title":"Investment over time: when is money available?","body":"The firm starts with 100 thousand dollars. Project A can start in years 1–4, matures two years later and returns principal plus 15%. B starts in year 3, matures at the end of year 5, returns 25% and is capped at 40 thousand. C starts in year 2, matures at the end of year 4, returns 40% and is capped at 30 thousand. D can start every year and returns 6% after one year.\nUse thousand-dollar units. Let Aₜ and Dₜ denote amounts invested at the beginning of year t, and B and C the one-time amounts. Maturity proceeds become available at the next year boundary. The budget constraints below prevent spending a payoff before it arrives.\n$$A_1+D_1=100$$\n$$A_2+C+D_2=1.06D_1$$\n$$A_3+B+D_3=1.15A_1+1.06D_2$$\n$$A_4+D_4=1.15A_2+1.06D_3$$\n$$D_5=1.15A_3+1.40C+1.06D_4$$\n$$0\\le B\\le40,\\quad0\\le C\\le30,\\quad A_t,D_t\\ge0.$$\nMaximize terminal wealth:\n$$W=1.15A_4+1.25B+1.06D_5.$$\nA 15% return means a 1.15 maturity multiplier, not 0.15. A flow diagram makes the timing clearer: an A investment skips one intermediate year boundary, while D links consecutive boundaries. With no cash reserve need and positive D returns, unused available funds can be put in D.","sections":[],"sources":["chapter04-2.pdf:25-26"]},{"id":"dea","title":"Data envelopment analysis: relative efficiency","body":"DEA compares units that use several inputs to produce several outputs. Here the units are Alton, Beeks, Carey and Delancey schools. Inputs are teacher/student ratio, supplementary funds per student and parents’ education level. Outputs are reading, mathematics and history test scores.\nA single output/input ratio is inadequate when both sides have several measures in different units. DEA searches for nonnegative weights that give each evaluated school its most favorable efficiency score, while preventing any school from scoring above one under the same weights.\nFor school o, let vᵢ weight inputs and uᵣ weight outputs. A standard constant-returns-to-scale multiplier formulation completes the blank model page in the deck:\n$$\\max\\sum_r u_r y_{ro}$$\n$$\\sum_i v_i x_{io}=1,\\qquad\\sum_r u_r y_{rj}-\\sum_i v_i x_{ij}\\le0\\quad\\text{for every school }j,$$\n$$u_r,v_i\\ge0.$$\nThe normalization turns the fractional efficiency ratio into a linear program. Solve separately for each school. A value below one indicates relative inefficiency under this chosen model; a value of one alone is not a claim that every input and output is individually efficient, especially when zero weights or slacks are allowed.\nThe slides do not specify a returns-to-scale convention on the blank formulation page. The displayed model uses the usual constant-returns assumption; adding restrictions on weights or choosing variable returns produces a different DEA model.","sections":[{"title":"Read the data before optimizing","body":"Alton uses no more of any input than Delancey (0.06 vs 0.06; 260 vs 460; 11.3 vs 13.1) and produces at least as much of every output (86 vs 81; 75 vs 73; 71 vs 69). Delancey is therefore dominated in the observed data. DEA is a comparison within the modeled set, not a causal explanation of school performance.\nThe closing slide cites an American Red Cross application integrating Excel, Access and Visual Basic to evaluate chapters with DEA. It illustrates turning many optimization models into a decision-support application."}],"sources":["chapter04-2.pdf:27-31"]},{"id":"uncertain-returns","title":"Uncertain returns: what the last slide does and does not specify","body":"The extension replaces A’s fixed return with 25% at probability 20% and 12.5% at probability 80%. Its expected return is 0.2×0.25 + 0.8×0.125 = 0.15. C returns 45% with probability 90% and −5% with probability 10%, giving expected return 0.40. D remains at 6%.\nFor B, the slide lists a 30% return with probability 40% and a 60% return with probability 21.67%. These probabilities total only 61.67%; the remaining outcome is not given. The listed contribution to expected return is approximately 0.25002, but that is not the full expectation without the missing outcome.\nAn expected-return model would explicitly choose a risk-neutral objective and require a complete probability specification. In a multistage model, later choices can depend only on information already observed, and future reinvestment must respect realized cash availability. Substituting expected multipliers into all cash-balance equations is not automatically equivalent to a valid stochastic investment plan.","sections":[],"sources":["chapter04-2.pdf:32"],"warning":"Chapter 4, page 32 leaves B’s probability distribution incomplete. No unique stochastic optimum is asserted here. A zero-return missing outcome would recover approximately 25% expected return, but that is an additional assumption, not stated slide content."}]}];
